 and Balances of the United States Government, page 512.\\n1/ Farm housing and other loan programs, and Farm Tenancy Mortgage Insurance Fund.\\n2/ Excludes securities issued under Defense Production Act.\\n3/ Includes notes issued to borrow for the urban renewal program (formerly slum clearance program); also includes loans for the prefabricated housing loans program from September 1950, when it was transferred from the Reconstruction Finance Corporation, through November 1954; and public facility loans beginning January 1956. Notes issued to borrow for the Federal National Mortgage Association from September 1950 through July 1954, are shown under the Association column.\\nb/ From September 1950 through July 1954, consists of notes of the Housing and Home Finance Administrator, issued to borrow for the Association. Beginning August 1954, consists of liabilities taken over by the Association from the Administrator in accordance with the act approved August 2, 1954, and notes issued by the Association for the authority of that act. U.S.C. 1720, 1723 (d); and beginning September 1954, also securities transferred from the Reconstruction Finance Corporation (see footnote 6). Prior to September 1950, the Association was financed from funds of the Reconstruction Finance Corporation, which owned the capital stock.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [570, 679, 1079, 1120], \"page_id\": 42}], \"content\": \"5/ and predecessor agencies. Beginning September 1956, figures exclude notes previously issued by the Administrator in connection with incremental media guarantees. The obligation of these notes was assumed by the Director of Defense Production Administration Agency, pursuant to the act of July 18, 1956 (22 U.S.C. 1142). The notes together with others issued for the same purpose are included in \\\"Other.\\\" \\n6/ Excludes securities issued under Defense Production Act of 1950, includes securities issued under Federal Civil Defense Act of 1950 through November 1953, after which they were taken over by the Secretary of the Treasury, pursuant to the act approved July 30, 1953 (15 U.S.C. 109). These are also included in \\\"Other.\\\" Under the act of November 1, 1954, September 1954, under Reorganization Plan No. 2 of 1954, the remaining securities issued by the Corporation to the Treasury were transferred as follows: $4.2 million to Export-Import Bank of Washington, $92 million to Federal National Mortgage Association, and $14 million to Small Business Administration. \\n7/ Consolidated notes issued by the Administrator, General Services Administration, beginning January 1951 (Administrator, Defense Materials Procurement Agency, January 1952 through July 1953) for defense materials procurement; Reconstruction Finance Corporation, January 1951 through September 1953 (after which its activities under this act were transferred to the Secretary of the Treasury); and the Secretary of the Treasury beginning July 1953 to the Secretary of the Interior (Defense Minerals Exploration Administration) beginning June 1951; the Export-Import Bank of Washington beginning April 1952; and the Secretary of Agriculture beginning June 1954. \\n8/ Consists of notes issued by Secretary of the Treasury beginning December 1953 (see footnote 6); Small Business Administration, September 1954 through April 1958; for informational media guarantees by the United States Government (agency budgetary control, September 1956 (see footnote 5); Secretary of Commerce (Maritime Administration) for Federal Ship Mortgage Insurance Fund beginning February 1959; and Virgin Islands Corporation beginning September 1959.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [10, 3, 162, 31], \"page_id\": 43}], \"content\": \"February 1960\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1038, 26, 1054, 42], \"page_id\": 43}], \"content\": \"29\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [418, 63, 656, 81], \"page_id\": 43}], \"content\": \"STATUTORY DEBT LIMITATION\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [10, 100, 504, 347], \"page_id\": 43}], \"content\": \"The Second Liberty Bond Act (31 U.S.C. 757 b), as amended by an act approved June 30, 1959, provides that the face amount of obligations issued under authority of that act, and the face amount of obligations guaranteed as to principal and interest by the United States (except guaranteed obligations held by the Secretary of the Treasury), shall not exceed in the aggregate $285 billion outstanding at any one time. The corresponding limitation in effect under the act of June 26, 1946, was $275 billion and that under the act of September 2, 1958, was $283 billion. In addition, temporary increases have been\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [558, 112, 1048, 356], \"page_id\": 43}], \"content\": \"authorized as follows: $6 billion beginning on August 28, 1954, and ending on June 30, 1956 (act approved August 28, 1954, and June 30, 1955); $3 billion beginning on July 1, 1956, and ending on June 30, 1957 (act approved July 9, 1956); $5 billion beginning on February 26, 1958, and ending on June 30, 1959 (act approved February 26, 1958); and $10 billion beginning on July 1, 1959, and ending on June 30, 1960 (act approved June 30, 1959). Obligations issued on a discount basis, and subject to redemption prior to maturity at the option of the owner, are included in the statutory debt limitation at current redemption values.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [267, 362, 794, 389], \"page_id\": 43}], \"content\": \"Table 1.- Status under Limitation, December 31, 1959\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [441, 393, 602, 412], \"page_id\":