{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_42183f97-1e07-4b74-bfc0-102d639f72c4","role":"user","created":1775379157,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_ee5b4c33657e431897dd16f4","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"37:U. S. Government Printing Office, Washington 25, D. C. Subscription per year $6.00 domestic, $7.50 foreign. Single copy price varies\n2429:17/ Holders of the L& notes, who have the option to redeem at par on February 15, 1960, by giving notice not later than November 16, 1959, were permitted to exchange their holdings on November 15, 1959, for the L-7/8% notes.\n2885:In the savings and loan associations, nonprofit institutions, corporate pension trust funds, dealers and brokers, and investments of foreign balances and international accounts in this country. Beginning December 1946, includes investments by the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development and the International Monetary Fund in special noninterest-bearing notes issued by the U. S. Government.\n3729:The first three sections which follow are published monthly. They provide summaries, by periods and by countries, of data on short-term banking liabilities to and claims on foreigners and transactions in long-term securities by foreigners, and present detailed breakdowns of the latest available preliminary data.\n3731:Section IV provides supplementary data in six tables which appear at less frequent intervals. Table 1, short-term foreign liabilities and claims reported by non-financial concerns, is published quarterly in the January, April, July, and October issues of the Bulletin. Table 2, long-term foreign liabilities and claims reported by banks and bankers, and Table 3, estimated gold reserves and dollar holdings of foreign countries and international institutions, are published quarterly in the March, June, September, and December issues. Table 4, foreign credit and debit balances in brokerage accounts, appears semi-annually in the March and September issues. Table 5, short-term liabilities to foreigners in countries and areas not regularly reported separately by banking institutions, is presented annually, appearing in the April issue through 1958 and in the March issue thereafter. Table 6, purchases and sales of long-term securities by foreigners during the preceding calendar year, also appears annually, beginning with the May 1959 issue.\n4422:| Country > Unnamed: 0_level_1 > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Purchases by foreigners > Total purchases > Unnamed: 1_level_2 | Purchases by foreigners > U.S. Government bonds and notes > Unnamed: 2_level_2 | Purchases by foreigners > Corporate and other > Bonde | Purchases by foreigners > Corporate and other > Stocks | Sales by foreigners > Foreign securities > Bonde | Sales by foreigners > Foreign securities > Stocks | Sales by foreigners > Total sales > Unnamed: 7_level_2 | Sales by foreigners > U.S. Government bonds and notes > Unnamed: 8_level_2 | Unnamed: 9_level_0 > Corporate and other > Bonde | Unnamed: 10_level_0 > Corporate and other > Stocks | Unnamed: 11_level_0 > Foreign securities > Bonde | Unnamed: 12_level_0 > Foreign securities > Stocks |\n4677:| Account | Total 1/2/ | Development loan fund | International Cooperation Administration Foreign investment guaranty fund | Atomic Energy Commission Defense production guaranteee | Export-Import Bank of Washington Regulier lending activities | Liquidation of certain Reconstruction Finance Corporation assets | Expansion of defense production |\n5730:| Account > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Total 1/ > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | International Cooperation Administration > U. S. dollar loans | International Cooperation Administration > Foreign currency loans | Export-Import Bank of Washington > Foreign currency loans 15/ | Veterans' Administration > Loan guaranty program | General Services Administration > Public Works Administration (in liquidation) | Agriculture Department > Farmers' Home Administration |\n6074:| Account | 